  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">End-term Evaluation of Health Resilience Fund (HRF, 2022-2026)</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">&lt;h2&gt;The purpose of the contract is to conduct a End -Term Evaluation of the ongoing multi-donor-funded HRF Programme ( July 2022 - June 2026).&lt;/h2&gt;&lt;p&gt;The purpose of the evaluation is to conduct an End of Project Evaluation of the multi-donor fund, the Health Resilience Fund (HRF: July 2022- 30 June 2026). Other donors supporting the HRF are the UK&#xbf;s Foreign, Commonwealth &amp; Development Office (FCDO), the Government of Ireland, and the Global Alliance for Vaccines and Immunisation (GAVI). The contract is executed through indirect management with the United Nations Children&apos;s Fund (UNICEF) as Fund Manager and implemented by UNICEF, The United Nations Population Fund (UNFPA), and the World Health Organisation (WHO).&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;The &lt;strong&gt;specific objectives&lt;/strong&gt; for this evaluation are:&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;1. To assess the extent to which the HRF has contributed towards the achievement of sustainable and equitable health outcomes to enhance Zimbabwe&#xbf;s human capital development towards a prosperous and empowered upper-middle society by 2030. This will include outputs e.g. vaccination rates and contribution to long-term impacts e.g. reduction in maternal mortality, within the HRF framework. The aim is to evaluated the HRF but the evaluation could also comment on the impact of the HRF and its predecessor HDF, and HRF on impact indicators e.g. MMR&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;2. To assess the extent to which the HRF has embraced the humanitarian/development nexus in this context of multi-layered hazards&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;3. To assess the extent to which the HRF has safeguarded gains achieved through the Health Transition Fund (2010-2015) and the Health Development Fund (2016-2021), and also ensured sustainability after the end of the HRF, by investing strategically to end preventable deaths, and consolidate Zimbabwe&#xbf;s Global Health Security systems, while strengthening the health system and advocating for enhanced allocative, technical, and operational efficiencies.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;4. To assess the extent to which gender considerations were integrated into the design, implementation, and outcomes of the HRF in Zimbabwe.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;As part of the ongoing program, HRF donors and stakeholders have requested an independent evaluation of the HRF program inclusive of program delivery of governance structures to document progress to date.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;h2&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;/h2&gt;</narrative>
  </description>
